This is a '''list of notable Italian [[soup|soups]]'''. Soups are sometimes served as the {{lang|it|[[Italian meal structure#Formal meal structure|primo]]}} ([[Course (food)|first course]]) in [[Italian cuisine]]. In some regions of [[Italy]], such as [[Veneto]], soup is eaten more than pasta.<ref name="Caggiano 2002 p. 20"/>

==Italian soups==
{{Dynamic list}}
* {{lang|it|[[Acquacotta]]}} – originally a [[Peasant foods|peasant food]], its preparation and consumption dates back to [[ancient history]].<ref name="Romer"/>
* {{lang|it|[[Bagnun]]}} – based mainly on [[Anchovy|anchovies]]
* {{lang|it|[[Buridda]]}} – a [[seafood]] soup or stew from [[Liguria]], northern Italy<ref name="Hoffmann" />
* {{lang|it|[[Garmugia]]}} – originated in [[Lucca]], [[Tuscany]], central Italy<ref name="Johns"/><ref name="Elon"/>
* {{lang|it|[[Ginestrata]]}} – originated in Tuscany, northern Italy, it can be described as a thin, lightly spiced [[Eggs as food|egg]]-based soup.<ref name="Solley"/><ref name="ICACH"/><ref name="Sinclair"/>
* [[Macaroni soup]] – a traditional dish in Italy that is sometimes served with beans, which is known as {{lang|it|[[pasta e fagioli]]}}<ref name="reiview">{{cite news|url=http://www.newsday.com/entertainment/dining/122193,0,7784884.venue|title=Cara Mia Due|publisher=Newsday|access-date=2008-08-06}}{{dead link|date=May 2026|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>
* {{lang|it|[[Maccu]]}} – a [[Sicilian cuisine|Sicilian]] soup and also a foodstuff that is prepared with dried and crushed [[Vicia faba|fava beans]] (also known as broad beans) and [[fennel]] as primary ingredients.<ref name="Helstosky"/><ref name="Sicily"/> It dates back to ancient history.<ref name="Helstosky"/><ref name="CR"/><ref name="Simeti"/>
* {{lang|it|[[Minestra di ceci]]}} – prepared with chickpeas as a main ingredient, it is a common soup in the [[Abruzzo]] region of central Italy.<ref>{{cite book | last1=De Vita | first1=O.Z. | last2=Fant | first2=M.B. | last3=Marini | first3=L. | last4=Hyers | first4=Gentl & | title=Sauces & Shapes: Pasta the Italian Way | publisher=W. W. Norton | year=2013 | isbn=978-0-393-08243-2 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=rUczAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA248 | page=248}}</ref><ref>{{cite book | last1=Talbott | first1=M. | last2=Schlechter | first2=A. | last3=Project | first3=Rome Sustainable Food | title=Zuppe | publisher=Little Bookroom | series=Rome sustainable food project | year=2012 | isbn=978-1-892145-97-0 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=oclaxumt6mwC&pg=PA95 | page=95}}</ref>
* {{lang|it|[[Wedding soup|Minestra maritata]]}} or [[Wedding soup|Italian wedding soup]]
* [[Minestrone]] – a thick soup made with vegetables, often with the addition of pasta or rice. Common ingredients include beans, onions, celery, carrots, stock, and tomatoes.
* {{lang|it|[[Panada]]}} – in northeastern Italy, it serves as an inexpensive meal in the poor areas of the countryside. It may be enriched with eggs, beef [[broth]], and grated cheese. It was frequently prepared as a meal for elderly or ill people.
* {{lang|it|[[Pappa al pomodoro]]}} – a thick [[Italian cuisine#Tuscany|Tuscan]] soup typically prepared with fresh tomatoes, bread, olive oil, garlic, basil, and various other fresh ingredients
* {{lang|it|[[Ribollita]]}} – a famous Tuscan [[soup]], a hearty [[Pottage|potage]] made with bread and vegetables.<ref name="zeldes">{{Cite web | last = Zeldes | first = Leah A. | title = Eat this! ''Ribollita,'' ribsticking winter 'soup' from Tuscany | work = Dining Chicago | publisher = Chicago's Restaurant & Entertainment Guide, Inc. | date = 2010-12-08 | url = http://www.diningchicago.com/blog/2010/12/08/eat-this-ribollita-ribsticking-winter-soup-from-tuscany/ | access-date = 2011-05-18 | archive-date = 2012-06-14 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20120614034522/http://www.diningchicago.com/blog/2010/12/08/eat-this-ribollita-ribsticking-winter-soup-from-tuscany/ | url-status = dead }}</ref> There are many variations, but the main ingredients always include leftover bread, [[Kidney bean#Classification|cannellini beans]] and inexpensive vegetables such as carrot, cabbage, beans, [[Chard|silverbeet]], {{lang|it|[[Lacinato kale|cavolo nero]]}}, and onion. Its name literally means 'reboiled'.
* {{lang|it|[[Sciusceddu]]}} – prepared using meatballs and eggs as primary ingredients
* {{lang|it|[[Stracciatella (soup)|Stracciatella]]}} – consists of meat broth and small shreds of an egg-based mixture, prepared by drizzling the mixture into boiling broth and stirring.
* [[Walnut soup]] – prepared in the [[Piedmont]] region of northern Italy, which has a significant amount of walnut groves<ref>{{cite book | author=The Caboto Club of Windsor | title=Cooking with Giovanni Caboto: Regional Italian Cuisine | publisher=Biblioasis | year=2012 | isbn=978-1-927428-05-4 | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=skuCCgAAQBAJ&pg=PA88 | access-date=May 28, 2017 | page=88}}</ref>
* {{lang|it|[[Zuppa alla modenese]]}} – made with stock, spinach, butter, salt, eggs, Parmesan cheese, nutmeg, and croutons<ref name="H. Bemporad & figlio 1902"/>
* {{lang|it|[[Zuppa del canavese]]}} – made from white stock, tomato puree, butter, carrot, celery, onion, cauliflower, bacon fat, Parmesan cheese, parsley, sage, salt, and pepper<ref>{{cite book|author=Mrs. W. G. Waters|title=The Cook's Decameron: A Study in Taste, Containing Over Two Hundred Recipes for Italian Dishes|url=https://archive.org/details/b21528536|access-date=31 May 2013|year=1901|publisher=W. Heinemann|location=London|page=[https://archive.org/details/b21528536/page/106 106]}}</ref>
* {{lang|it|[[Zuppa toscana]]}} – made with [[Sausages in Italian cuisine|sausage]], crushed red peppers, diced white onion, bacon, garlic puree, chicken bouillon, heavy cream, and potatoes
* Pescatore soup 

